00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:41.480
Those parts feel like misfits, a flaw in

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:43.680
your resume. But here's the thing that I

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:46.480
learned. If you

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:47.920
look at a guitar.

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:52.000
I have a guitar.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:54.000
It has six strings. Let's see.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:56.480
Here it is.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:58.880
And these six strings have different

00:23:56.480 --> 00:24:03.880
thickness, different tension.

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:03.880
Each one is tuned to a different note.

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:11.920
Now, if you strum them open,

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:14.520
it's just noise.

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:17.200
It doesn't sound musical

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:19.760
unless it sounds musical to you. Most

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:22.280
people will say, "Hmm,

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:26.000
what is that?" But if you learn to press

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:26.000
at certain points,

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:45.080
sounds gorgeous, right? Now,

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:47.840
I play guitar like a drummer would play

00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:51.880
guitar. These are called cowboy chords,

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:53.640
anyone can learn them. But the point is

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:56.440
each string

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:59.800
resonates with the other,

00:24:56.440 --> 00:25:03.360
and that's how harmony is produced. And

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:06.400
therein lies the trap and the solution.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:08.960
If you bring just one string

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:12.080
to your dream and to your work

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:14.800
and leave the rest of them at home, then

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:17.520
